  "result": "I graded Alex's quiz 0001 submission and wrote the grades to `grades/0001-s1.json`.\n\n| Item | Verdict | Feedback |\n|---|---|---|\n| q1 | Wrong (M1) | You picked \"the student is excluded\", which is what an INNER JOIN does. A LEFT JOIN keeps the 4th student, with NULL in the enrollment columns. |\n| q2 | Correct | 3 rows is right. Only students with a match survive an INNER JOIN. |\n| q3 | Partial | Your INNER JOIN versus LEFT JOIN explanation is right. The unmatched columns hold NULL, not \"blank\". NULL means no value, and a blank is a real value. |\n\nYour q3 answer says a LEFT JOIN keeps unmatched students, but in q1 you chose the INNER JOIN behaviour. The idea isn't reliable yet when you apply it to a query.\n\n**Next step:** Take two small tables with 4 students and 3 enrollments. Write out the result rows by hand for an INNER JOIN and then a LEFT JOIN, and mark where the NULLs appear. After that, we can move on to duplicate rows and COUNT.\n\nI also added `learning-records/0002-left-join-unmatched-rows.md` and updated `NOTES.md`.\n\nDo you want a short lesson on predicting LEFT JOIN results, with that practice built in?",
